	 *...*'}, {'BodyText': 'The programmer attempts to avoid XSS exploits (CWE-79) by encoding the POST values so they will not be interpreted as valid HTML. However, the htmlentities() encoding is not appropriate when the data are used as HTML attributes, allowing more attributes to be injected.'}, {'BodyText': 'For example, an attacker can set picAltText to:'}, {'Nature': 'Attack', 'ExampleCode': '```
	"altTextHere' onload='alert(document.cookie)"
```'}, {'BodyText': 'This will result in the generated HTML image tag:'}, {'Nature': 'Result', 'Language': 'HTML', 'ExampleCode': "```
	<img src='pic.jpg' alt='altTextHere' onload='alert(document.cookie)' />
```"}, {'BodyText': 'The attacker can inject arbitrary javascript into the tag due to this incorrect encoding.'}]}]

[{'Entries': [{'IntroText': 'The following example assigns some character values to a list of characters and prints them each individually, and then as a string. The third character value is intended to be an integer taken from user input and converted to an int.'}, {'Nature': 'Bad', 'Language': 'C', 'ExampleCode': '```
	char *foo;
	foo=malloc(sizeof(char)*5);
	foo[0]='a';
	foo[1]='a';
	foo[2]=atoi(getc(stdin));
	foo[3]='c';
	foo[4]='\0'
	printf("%c %c %c %c %c \n",foo[0],foo[1],foo[2],foo[3],foo[4]);
	printf("%s\n",foo);
```'}, {'BodyText': 'The first print statement will print each character separated by a space. However, if a non-integer is read from stdin by getc, then atoi will not make a conversion and return 0. When foo is printed as a string, the 0 at character foo[2] will act as a NULL terminator and foo[3] will never be printed.'}]}]

[{'ID': 'DX-2', 'Entries': [{'IntroText': 'The following code takes untrusted input and uses a regular expression to filter "../" from the input. It then appends this result to the /home/user/ directory and attempts to read the file in the final resulting path.'}, {'Nature': 'Bad', 'Language': 'Perl', 'ExampleCode': '```
	my $Username = GetUntrustedInput();
	$Username =~ s/\.\.\///;
	my $filename = "/home/user/" . $Username;
	ReadAndSendFile($filename);
```'}, {'BodyText': 'Since the regular expression does not have the /g global match modifier, it only removes the first instance of "../" it comes across. So an input value such as:'}, {'Nature': 'Attack', 'ExampleCode': '```
	../../../etc/passwd
```'}, {'BodyText': 'will have the first "../" stripped, resulting in:'}, {'Nature': 'Result', 'ExampleCode': '```
	../../etc/passwd
```'}, {'BodyText': 'This value is then concatenated with the /home/user/ directory:'}, {'Nature': 'Result', 'ExampleCode': '```
	/home/user/../../etc/passwd
```'}, {'BodyText': 'which causes the /etc/passwd file to be retrieved once the operating system has resolved the ../ sequences in the pathname. This leads to relative path traversal (CWE-23).'}]}]

